{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for task/resident-100x at exact repaired head 65ab3f68ad64f47db9ecfe80857d879d41ee233e. Head and clean status verified before and after every mutation; probes deleted, worktree unchanged. Gates green on the pristine tree: vet, build, test, and -race 12/12. Disclosure: a delegate ran the sweep under my direction; I reproduced the blocker's closure and the sharpest new finding myself before signing.\n\nAll six prior findings are genuinely closed, and F1 I confirmed live rather than by fixture, since that is how I reported it. Rotating .git/gitseq/sequencer under a running gs serve and submitting now yields exit 1 with \"No principal matched\", the sequence ref is byte-identical before and after at b46ec3f913fe914e15b8850cced7847e9aa2128c, and the resident still answers depth 2. Refused before CAS, which is strictly better than main e5ee7a0, where the ref advanced and only the read path complained. Forcing the ref onto a wrongly-signed commit to drive the warm delta path also refuses. F2 and F3 are closed by their own mutations now failing, F5 is closed and complete with no other resident retention path, and F4 is closed more strongly than I asked: the fixture is a real 351-commit signed chain, and making scanAfter delegate to scanHead collapses the delta arm exactly onto cold (115ms to 1775ms against cold 1777ms) and hard-fails the benchmark's own cold-versus-delta equality assertion. It detects its own subject being removed, which is precisely what the old fixture could not do. Decision equivalence against main holds byte-for-byte at all 399 prefixes of the real log, delta equals full at every prefix, and twelve concurrent acts across resident and external workspaces under -race produce no skipped or duplicated records.\n\nThe verdict is narrow, and it is this: the same technique that found F2 and F3 last round still finds four integrity checks with zero test coverage, in the very same functions. Each mutation below leaves go test over kernel, app and workroom fully green.\n\nThe one that decides the verdict is the delta parent-chain check at kernel.go:612. I ran this myself: replacing `len(parents) != 1 || parents[0] != expectedParent` with a condition that never fires leaves the entire suite passing. That guard is what makes \"rewinds and non-descendant heads force a full audit\" true, and it is a stated condition of this task. The existing TestReaderFallsBackToFullAuditAfterRefRewind cannot reach it, because it rewinds to a strict ancestor via UpdateRef(ref, first.Head, second.Head), where the rev-list range is empty and a different guard fires first. Rewind to a sibling instead and the neutered code takes the delta path and reports Depth 3 for a two-event chain — a silently accepted sequence fork, which is the same severity class as the F1 blocker this round repaired. RevListAfter's own doc comment tells callers not to omit this check; nothing enforces that.\n\nThree more, all real gaps rather than equivalent mutants, each with a probe that passes on HEAD and kills the mutant. Deleting the base.Dedup conflict block in scanAfter is green, after which an externally appended commit replaying an existing idempotency key is accepted by the delta path while a cold Verify rejects it — full and delta disagree, which the branch claims they cannot. Merging additions inside the scan loop rather than after it is green, after which a two-commit delta whose second commit fails admits one dedup entry from the failed prefix; the code comment explicitly claims that invariant and the existing wrong-sequencer test uses a one-commit delta, so it cannot reach the line. And dropping `|| start \u003c 0` in Snapshot at app.go:635 is green while the state is reachable through local optimistic apply, ref rewind and sibling advance, where the mutant panics on a negative slice bound — the same class as F3, one function over.\n\nTwo notes rather than findings. kernel.Create never checks that the signing key's public half equals desc.SequencerPublicKey, the same footgun F1 closed for submit; not exploitable today because app.Init derives both from one keygen and a mismatch fails every audit at genesis, but unguarded. And the F1 verify costs about 21ms per resident append, roughly 25 percent, which is the right price to pay — worth recording only so the branch's headline number is read as a read-path result rather than a write-path one.\n\nI want to be clear that this is not a complaint about the code. Everywhere I attacked the implementation it held: live key rotation into both submit and warm-delta paths, attempts to make the fold mutate its caller's records through the new nil-out, attempts to corrupt an already-returned projection with a later append, and the search for a ref-advancing path that bypasses the new verify all failed. The two terminal-head assertions in scanAfter and scanHead are genuine equivalent mutants and should not be \"fixed\". What is missing is coverage on four guards whose removal a reader cannot detect, on a branch whose central claim is that a fast path stays decision-equivalent to a slow one. Add the four tests and this is approvable."}